WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.210
SPEAKER: Apigee X allows you to
expose your APIs to end users

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.460
by implementing API proxies.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.810
API proxies are nothing but a
wrapper for your actual APIs.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.640
Did you know that
the API proxy must be

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.250
deployed for it to be invoked?

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.560
The process of
deploying the API proxy

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.020
and making it accessible
to the end users

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.570
is referred to as
API proxy deployment.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.550
Before you deploy
your API proxy,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.910
you need to complete a set
of prerequisites correctly.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.130
Networking and
routing configurations

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:39.690
are two of the key prerequisites
for API proxy deployment.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:42.510
A key point to note, if
the configurations are not

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.980
defined correctly, then your API
proxies may not be accessible.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.370
In this video, we will learn
the Apigee X networking

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.790
architecture and demonstrate
how to verify the networking

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.910
configuration of an
Apigee X organization.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.220
On a high level, there
are three key things

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.470
to be configured in networking.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:06.170
First, the Virtual
Private Clouds, or VPCs.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.570
Apigee X instances
are proficient in

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.000
internet Google-managed VPCs.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:13.490
However, Google-managed
VPCs are not

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.670
directly accessible
from your GCP projects.

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:20.180
Therefore, you need to
pair them either with VPCs

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.890
or shared with VPCs created
in your GCP projects.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:29.060
Secondly, the Managed
Instance Group, or MIG.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.720
Currently, GCP load balances
cannot directly route requests

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.730
to Google managed VPCs.

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.580
Therefore, you need to
use a MIG for forwarding

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.590
requests received
by the load balancer

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.990
to the Apigee X instance.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:46.190
Be aware that this MIG needs
to be created in the same VPC

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.160
network attached to the
Apigee X organization

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.920
and an ingress firewall
rule is required

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.700
for allowing the load balancer
to route requests to the MIG.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.130
Finally, the load balancer.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.140
Depending on your
routing requirements,

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.440
you could either choose an
external or an internal load

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:08.460
balancer for routing requests
to the Apigee X instances.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.370
Keep in mind that, if you
are using a shared VPC,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.000
then ensure that all managed
instance groups, client hosts,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.210
and target hosts are
created in the same network

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.580
as shown in this diagram.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.700
All right, let's jump into
the Google Cloud Console

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.790
and verify the networking
configuration in a sample

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.010
Apigee X organization.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.980
First off, let's verify
if the proper VPC

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.870
network is attached to a
sample Apigee X organization.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.670
Open the Cloud Shell.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.470
Execute the
following API request

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.470
to determine the VPC network
attached to the Apigee X

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.300
organization.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.880
The authorized network
property in this output

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.750
refers to the name
of the attached VPC.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:55.470
If you are using a shared VPC,
ensure that authorized network

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.920
property has the name
of the actual VPC that

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.430
is attached to the GCB project
in which the load balancer

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.900
and the MIGs are created.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.210
Next up, to verify the
configuration of the MIG.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.210
Go to Compute Engine
Instance Groups page

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.670
and select your MIG.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.980
Click on the Instance Template.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.460
Scroll down to the network tag
section and note down the tags.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.120
Next, ensure the VPC network
defined in the network

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.690
interfaces section is the
same as the VPC attached

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:33.890
to the Apigee X organization.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.950
Scroll down further to the
custom metadata section

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.430
and verify the endpoint IP
address against the Apigee X

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:43.040
instance IP address.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.160
Navigate to the VPC
network Firewall page

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.310
and verify the
ingress firewall rule.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.410
Be aware, this
firewall rule needs

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:54.500
to be created in the same
VPC attached to the Apigee X

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.330
organization.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.970
Ensure that the
network tags of the MIG

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.380
are defined under target tags
and allow the load balancer

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.870
health check probs, source
IP addresses ranges,

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.940
are included under the
source filters section.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.810
Finally, let's look at how
to verify the load balancing

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.550
configuration.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:16.010
Go to Network services
load balancing

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.740
and click on the
load balancer name.

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:21.050
Under the front
end section, verify

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:24.980
that protocol, IP address,
port, and the TLS certificate

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.710
are configured correctly.

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:30.790
Next, if any host and
path rules are defined,

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.550
verify whether they are
configured correctly.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.080
Lastly, under the
Backend section,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.500
verify that the load
balancer backend is healthy

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:42.980
and it is pointing to the right
MIG, which forwards traffic

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.720
to the Apigee X instance.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.630
To summarize, you learned
the networking architecture

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.590
in Apigee X and how to verify
the networking configuration,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.310
namely, VPC, MIG,
and load balances

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:58.320
are configured correctly in
an Apigee X organization.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.370
[MUSIC PLAYING]

